1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:08,800
每当你与 AI 对话，无论你输入到 ChatGPT 的每一个问题，还是从 Gemini 或 Claude 获取的每一条回答，你都在使用一个源自
Every time you talk to an AI, every question you type into ChatGPT, and every answer you get from Gemini or Claude, you're using an idea from

2
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:17,188
单一论文中的想法。这是一篇由八名在 Google 工作的人撰写的科研论文，他们当时大部分只是想让 Google Translate 变得稍微好一点点。
one single paper. One research paper written by eight people who worked at Google who were mostly just trying to make Google translate a little bit better.

3
00:00:17,614 --> 00:00:26,320
今天我要给你们讲 Transformer 是如何被发明的故事。这非常不可思议，因为我觉得现在的技术似乎已经没有什么能被称为真正突破性的了。
Today I'm going to tell you the story of how the Transformer came to be invented, and it's pretty wild because I feel like nothing these days is really that groundbreaking.

4
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:36,036
并不是说我们每年都在发明灯泡或电话，不是的。但我认为这项发明属于伟大的成就之一，在未来的很多年里都会被反复提及。
And it's not like we are inventing the light bulb or the telephone every year, no. But I feel this invention is up there among the greats and will be referred to in many years to come.

5
00:00:36,334 --> 00:00:42,052
要理解为什么这如此重要，你必须了解在它出现之前，机器是如何阅读语言的。
To understand why this is so significant, you have to understand how machines used to read language before it.

6
00:00:42,606 --> 00:00:45,920
旧系统一次只能处理一个词。
Old systems would take in text one word at a time.

7
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:49,680
就像你可以想象单词正穿过一根吸管一样。
Kind of like you would imagine words passing through a straw.

8
00:00:49,870 --> 00:00:59,840
也就是说，一个词通过并存入内存，然后下一个词通过并存入内存，以此类推。而且这个过程是从左到右进行的。
So one word passes through, gets saved in memory, then the next word passes, gets saved in memory, and so on. And this would happen from left to right.

9
00:01:00,494 --> 00:01:04,239
这个过程实际上有一个名字，叫做循环网络。
This process actually has a name and it's called recurrent networks.

10
00:01:04,366 --> 00:01:13,668
虽然当时广泛使用——我想是因为当时没有其他选择，而且也没人质疑过这个系统——但这个系统确实有缺陷。
And whilst vastly used at the time, I guess because there was no alternative and no one had questioned this system so far, this system definitely had its flaws.

11
00:01:14,190 --> 00:01:17,040
其中一个主要的缺陷就是这个系统容易遗忘。
One major one being that the system was forgetful.

12
00:01:17,358 --> 00:01:22,436
当模型读到长句子的末尾时，开头的内容就已经变得模糊不清了。
By the time the model reached the end of a long sentence, the beginning had already gone fuzzy.

13
00:01:22,990 --> 00:01:29,284
所以要把段落开头的想法和段落末尾的意图联系起来，真的非常困难。
So connecting an idea at the start of a paragraph to one at the end of the paragraph was genuinely hard.

14
00:01:29,646 --> 00:01:33,412
第二个问题是每一步都依赖于前一步。
And the second issue is that each step depended on the one before it.

15
00:01:33,998 --> 00:01:38,148
这意味着即使你想这样做，你也无法同时运行所有步骤。
This means that even if you wanted to, you couldn't run all the steps at once.

16
00:01:38,638 --> 00:01:42,160
你必须一个接一个地完成每一个步骤。
You had to march through each step one by one.

17
00:01:42,382 --> 00:01:43,940
也就是说按顺序读完整个句子。
So through the sentence in order.

18
00:01:44,654 --> 00:01:53,412
正如你可以想象的那样，这让整个过程变得非常、非常、非常缓慢。不仅是阅读句子，还有训练这些模型。
As you can imagine, this made the whole process very, very, very slow. Not only for reading sentences, but also for training these models.

19
00:01:53,614 --> 00:01:58,660
而且也不是说你只要堆更多的算力就能解决问题。那没用的。
And it's not like you could just throw more computing power at it either. It wouldn't make a difference.

20
00:01:58,990 --> 00:02:03,360
在第 4 个词处理完之前，第 5 个词根本无法计算。
Word 5 genuinely can't be computed until word 4 is done.

21
00:02:03,694 --> 00:02:06,596
所以增加更多 GPU 并不能加快处理一个句子的速度。
So more GPUs don't speed up processing one sentence.

22
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:14,308
总之，这群由八名工程师组成的团队以及他们整个语言学领域，确实一直被困在用这种系统里。
So anyway, this group of eight engineers and their whole field of linguistics really were kind of stuck using this system.

23
00:02:14,606 --> 00:02:20,260
我不确定具体发生了什么，但我猜他们开始质疑为什么会采用这种顺序处理的方法。
And I'm not sure what happened exactly, but I guess they started questioning why this sequential approach existed.

24
00:02:21,006 --> 00:02:23,680
我的意思是，为什么模型要一个词一个词地读？
I mean, why does a model read one word at a time?

25
00:02:23,886 --> 00:02:28,196
如果模型能一次性看整个句子，不是更好吗？
Wouldn't it be better if the model could look at the entire sentence at once?

26
00:02:28,654 --> 00:02:30,596
也就是说同时处理每一个词。
So every word simultaneously.

27
00:02:31,214 --> 00:02:39,920
所以，你知道的，就是把所有单词全部拿出来，并为每个词找出模型应该关注哪些其他词。
So you know, in just so all, all the words, and for each word, figure out which other words the model should pay attention to.

28
00:02:40,206 --> 00:02:48,900
现在，很棒的一点是，这种关注其他词的机制实际上在这些旧的逐词处理模型中已经作为辅助功能存在了。
Now, what is great is that actually this mechanism to pay attention to other words already existed as a helper in these old word-by-word models.

29
00:02:49,280 --> 00:02:52,880
而且非常贴切地，它被叫做「注意力」。
And pretty fittingly it was called attention.

30
00:02:53,102 --> 00:02:56,484
那么，让我们通过看一个句子来更好地理解一下这个理论。
So let's understand this theory a little bit better by looking at a sentence.

31
00:02:56,974 --> 00:03:00,100
那只动物没过马路，因为它太累了。
The animal didn't cross the street because it was too tired.

32
00:03:00,686 --> 00:03:05,520
现在，当你读到这句话时，作为人类，我们知道它指代什么。
Now, when you read the sentence, we as humans know what it refers to.

33
00:03:05,870 --> 00:03:07,716
我们知道“它”指的是那只动物。
We know refers to the animal.

34
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:16,452
但电脑逐字阅读时可能会认为它指代的是马路，仅仅是因为它是离“it”这个词最近的名词。
But a computer reading word by word might think it refers to street, purely because it's the closest noun to the word it.

35
00:03:16,720 --> 00:03:26,160
注意力机制正是让模型能做出和你一样的判断，无论距离多远，都能把它与动物联系起来。
The mechanism of attention is what lets the model make the same decision as you, linking it to animal, no matter how far apart they are.

36
00:03:26,320 --> 00:03:28,836
于是，这八位 Google 研究人员产生了一个想法。
So these eight Google researchers had an idea.

37
00:03:29,120 --> 00:03:33,280
他们已经有了这个机制，现在只需要进一步完善他们的想法。
They had the mechanism, and now they just needed to develop their idea further.

38
00:03:33,486 --> 00:03:42,720
其中一位 Google 研究员，一个叫雅各布·乌什科里特的家伙，决定把这个辅助工具，换句话说，就是注意力机制，彻底剥离出来
One of the Google researchers, a guy named Jacob Ushkoret, decided to fully rip out the helper, or in other words, the mechanism of attention

39
00:03:42,894 --> 00:03:45,348
并独立出来单独使用。
and isolate it to use it on its own.

40
00:03:45,550 --> 00:03:50,180
这本质上是个正确的想法，但事实证明，执行起来并不那么简单。
This was essentially the right idea, but executing it wasn't that straightforward, it turns out.

41
00:03:50,478 --> 00:04:00,480
实际上是诺姆·沙兹，当时的一位同事，据我了解，他也是 Google 内部的一个传奇人物，他成功地将理论付诸实践。
It was in fact Noam Shazir, another co worker at the time, and a little bit of a legend around Google, from what I gather, that managed to turn the theory into practice.

42
00:04:01,102 --> 00:04:08,228
现在，我不想让诺姆·沙兹看起来比其他伙伴更受重视，因为他们确实并没有这样做。
Now, I don't want it to sound like I'm giving Noam Shazir any more credit than any of the other guys, because they certainly didn't.

43
00:04:08,558 --> 00:04:18,832
事实上，在撰写这篇论文时，参与这个想法的八位研究员都平分了功劳，甚至随机打乱了署名顺序，
In fact, when it came to writing the paper on this, each of the eight researchers who worked on the idea gave themselves equal credit and even scrambled the order randomly,

44
00:04:18,992 --> 00:04:20,676
作为一个真正的集体在工作。
working as a true collective.

45
00:04:21,390 --> 00:04:27,232
甚至当时的一名实习生，一个叫艾丹·戈麦兹的家伙，也被列为同等贡献者。
Even the intern at the time, a guy called Aidan Gomez, is listed as an equal contributor.

46
00:04:27,392 --> 00:04:37,632
而这篇论文，这篇现在成为本世纪引用量最高的 AI 或机器学习论文之一的论文，它讨论了注意力机制
And this paper, this paper that is now one of the most cited AI or machine learning papers of the century, the one that talks about the mechanism of attention

47
00:04:37,966 --> 00:04:43,072
是改进语言模型所真正需要的全部，而且它的名字非常出色。
being all you really need to improve language models, has the best name ever.

48
00:04:43,312 --> 00:04:49,956
如果你是披头士乐队的粉丝并熟悉他们的歌曲，你一定会觉得这个论文标题非常贴切。
If you are a Beatles fan and are familiar with their songs, you will definitely appreciate how apt the title of this paper is.

49
00:04:50,414 --> 00:04:53,392
它叫《Attention Is All You Need》。
It's called Attention is All You Need.

50
00:04:53,902 --> 00:04:54,352
懂了吗？
Get it?

51
00:04:54,542 --> 00:04:56,592
因为注意力机制？
Because of the attention mechanism?

52
00:04:56,846 --> 00:05:05,284
总之，论文正在撰写过程中，研究人员的截止日期临近，他们基本上是在描述一种特定架构的工作原理。
Anyway, so the paper is in the process of being written and their researchers deadline is looming, and essentially they are describing how a specific architecture works.

53
00:05:05,712 --> 00:05:13,924
而这个架构需要一个名字，所以雅各布·乌什科里特选择了 Transformer，据我所知，没别的原因，只是他觉得这个词听起来很酷。
And this architecture needed a name, so Jacob Ushkorit went with Transformer for no other reason that he just likes how it sounds,

54
00:05:14,286 --> 00:05:15,152
据我了解的情况。
from what I understand.

55
00:05:15,406 --> 00:05:19,524
我是说，确实听起来挺酷的，也许他是某种大黄蜂的粉丝，谁知道呢。
I mean, Farr it does sound pretty cool, maybe he was a bumblebee fan, who knows.

56
00:05:19,872 --> 00:05:21,792
现在他们有了论文。
So now they have the paper.

57
00:05:22,094 --> 00:05:25,712
他们描述了架构，是时候进行测试了。
They have described the architecture, time to put it to the test.

58
00:05:25,872 --> 00:05:31,952
因为这些研究人员，如果你还记得的话，最初是为了让 Google Translate 变得更快。
Because these researchers, if you remember, were originally looking at making Google translate faster.

59
00:05:32,206 --> 00:05:35,748
还有什么更好的办法来验证 Transformer 能否解决他们的问题呢？
What better way to see if the Transformer was able to fix their problem?

60
00:05:36,462 --> 00:05:37,952
所以他们进行了测试。
So they put it to the test.

61
00:05:38,222 --> 00:05:41,152
他们用旧模型和新的 Transformer 进行了对比。
They ran the old model against the new Transformer.

62
00:05:41,312 --> 00:05:46,592
你敢相信吗？Transformer 不仅仅是赢了，简直是碾压式的胜利。
And would you believe the Transformer didn't just beat it, it totally demolished it.

63
00:05:46,830 --> 00:05:55,392
它不仅击败了旧系统，而且在仅用极短时间训练和测试后，就击败了世界上最顶尖的系统。
Not only did it beat the old system, it beat the best systems in the world when put to the test and trained in just a fraction of the time.

64
00:05:55,632 --> 00:06:00,272
这些研究人员非常兴奋。我的意思是，这是一个巨大的、重大的突破。
These researchers were pretty ecstatic. I mean, this was a major, major breakthrough.

65
00:06:00,462 --> 00:06:03,872
于是他们完成了论文，并基本上向全世界发布了它。
So they finished the paper and essentially published it to the world.

66
00:06:04,078 --> 00:06:09,952
这部分让我有点难过，因为我能体会到他们的痛苦。因为说实话，当时根本没人在意这篇论文。
Now this part makes me a little bit sad because I feel their pain. Because honestly, no one really cared about this paper.

67
00:06:10,112 --> 00:06:15,632
因为从表面上看，这只是一篇关于翻译的论文，而且挺枯燥的，技术性很强且领域很窄。
Because on the surface, it was a translation paper and it was pretty boring, it was pretty technical and narrow.

68
00:06:15,790 --> 00:06:20,112
事实证明，讨论如何更快地翻译单词并不是什么酷炫的事。
And turns out talking about translating words faster isn't exactly sexy.

69
00:06:20,272 --> 00:06:30,432
但好在那些读到这篇论文的研究人员完全领会了其中的精髓，他们明白并行处理系统比一个词一个词地处理要好得多。
But luckily, the researchers who did read it totally got it, they totally understood how running a system in parallel is much better than running a system one word

70
00:06:30,702 --> 00:06:31,232
一次只处理一个词。
at a time.

71
00:06:31,406 --> 00:06:34,628
并行运行系统意味着你可以扩展它。
Running a system in parallel meant you could scale it.

72
00:06:34,926 --> 00:06:45,124
你可以把它做得更大，喂给它更多互联网数据，投入更多的电脑去跑，而且它不会崩溃，只会变得越来越强。
You could make it bigger, feed it more of the internet, throw more computers at it, and it wouldn't crash, it would just keep getting better and better and better.

73
00:06:45,614 --> 00:06:55,712
所以这事儿发生在 2017 年，就在不到一年的时间里，Transformer 就已经为这类新模型的诞生铺平了道路。
So this happened in 2017, and pretty much within a year, the Transformer had already paved the way for the first of this new kind of models to be created.

74
00:06:55,918 --> 00:07:03,012
这些模型能够读写语言，其中一个就叫 GPT，也就是 Generative Pre-Trained Transformer。
These models could read and write language, and one of them was called GPT or Generative Pre-Trained Transformer.

75
00:07:03,472 --> 00:07:10,672
没错，那个 T，就是你每次说 ChatGPT 时都会提到的那个字母，代表的就是 Transformer。
And yes, that T, the one you say every time you say ChatGPT, stands for Transformer.

76
00:07:11,054 --> 00:07:19,792
而且它并不局限于聊天机器人，现在你能想到的几乎每一个主流 AI 系统，比如 GPT-4、Gemini、Claude、Llama 等等，
And it didn't just stop at chatbots, nearly every major AI system you can name today, so GPT-4, Gemini, Court, Llama, you name it,

77
00:07:19,952 --> 00:07:21,572
底层架构都是 Transformer。
is underneath a transformer.

78
00:07:22,030 --> 00:07:32,548
所以想想看，生成图像的模型，没错，写代码的模型也是，甚至是预测几乎所有已知蛋白质结构的 AI 系统，
So think about it, the models that generate images, tick, the models that write code, them too, and even the AI that predicted the structure of nearly every protein known to science.

79
00:07:32,912 --> 00:07:38,660
只要你深入研究底层，你会反复看到 Transformer 架构。
Look under the hood, and again and again and again you will find the transformer architecture.

80
00:07:39,406 --> 00:07:47,472
但我们的故事还没到此为止，因为有一个巨大的反转：这八位研究人员最后全都离开了 Google。
But our story doesn't stop there, no, because in a major twist, all eight of the researchers ended up leaving Google.

81
00:07:47,632 --> 00:07:51,908
他们创立或加入了现在在 AI 领域家喻户晓的知名公司。
They founded or joined companies that today are recognizable names in the AI space.

82
00:07:52,352 --> 00:08:02,592
有一位共同创立了 Cohere，一位共同创立了 Character AI，一位去了 OpenAI，还有些人创办了 Adept 和 Essential AI，以及 Sakana
One co-founded Cohere, one co-founded Character AI, one went to OpenAI, others started Adept and Essential AI, and Sakana

83
00:08:02,752 --> 00:08:03,872
AI 在东京。
AI in Tokyo.

84
00:08:04,032 --> 00:08:05,444
甚至还有一个区块链平台。
And even a blockchain platform.

85
00:08:05,632 --> 00:08:13,552
换句话说，这篇由 Google 员工撰写的论文，本质上创造了 Google 目前最大的竞争对手。
So, in other words, this paper, the one that was written by Google employees, essentially created Google's biggest rivals.

86
00:08:13,742 --> 00:08:15,952
这简直是“亲手造出自己的对手”。
Talk about being the creator of your endeavor.

87
00:08:16,192 --> 00:08:23,044
所以，这就是 Transformer 的起源故事，以及那篇名为《Attention Is All You Need》的研究论文。
So, yes, this was the story of the Transformer and how it came about, as well as the research paper called Attention Is All You Need.

